# Environment

**Use PowerShell commands only. Assume Windows 11.** Do not use bash, `cat`, `ls`, or other Linux commands. Use `Get-Content` instead of `cat`, `Get-ChildItem` instead of `ls`, `Remove-Item` instead of `rm`, etc.

## Git workflow

When asked to review changes and push, always split changes into multiple logical commits grouped by concern instead of one large commit. Each group should be self-contained with a descriptive conventional-commit message (feat:, fix:, chore:, test:, etc.). Example groupings:

- **dependencies** — package.json + lockfile changes only
- **library/core module** — a new .ts file + its tests
- **UI page** — a route page + related component changes (e.g. sidebar nav link)
- **independent features** — separate commits for unrelated additions

After all commits are made, push once. This is the default behavior — no need for the user to ask explicitly.

### Pre-push quality gate (client-side)

**Run `pnpm prepush` before every push.** This executes two local checks that mirror CI:

1. `pnpm check-pr` — runs `scripts/check-pr-quality.mjs`, which verifies:
   - `CHANGELOG.md` is in the diff (must have an entry)
   - At least one `docs/` file is modified, OR the latest commit message contains `## Documentation`
2. `pnpm precheck` — runs `scripts/check-vendor.mjs` (already existed)

If `pnpm prepush` fails, fix the issue before pushing. Do NOT push with failing pre-checks. These are the same checks that will fail in CI as `check-changelog` and `check-docs`.

## GitHub tooling: MCP vs `gh` CLI

A GitHub MCP server (`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`) is configured in `cline_mcp_settings.json`. **Prefer MCP over `gh` CLI for everything except Project V2 operations.**

| Capability | MCP (use_mcp_tool) | `gh` CLI (execute_command) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Issues — create, update, read, comment, search, labels | ✅ All | ⚠️ JSON parsing |
| Pull requests — create, update, diff, merge, reviews, comments | ✅ All | ⚠️ JSON parsing |
| Repos — search, fork, contents, branches, tags, releases | ✅ All | ⚠️ |
| Code/commit/issue search | ✅ `search_code`, `search_issues`, etc. | ❌ |
| Files — create/update/delete, push multiple | ✅ `create_or_update_file`, `push_files` | ❌ |
| **Project V2** — draft items, status, fields | ❌ Not supported | ✅ Only option |
| Issue body editing | ✅ `issue_write` with `issue_number` | ✅ `gh issue edit` |

### Default rule

Use the **GitHub MCP server** for all issue/PR/repo operations. Fall back to `gh` CLI only for Project V2 drafting and status changes.

## GitHub Project workflow

We track planned work in a GitHub Project (V2). Project operations go through `gh`, not MCP.

## Project identity

- **Project:** holmgard-lore-editor development
- **Project number:** 3
- **Owner:** FrozenRegister
- **Project node ID:** `PVT_kwHOEMCAyM4BZ3n-`
- **URL:** <https://github.com/users/FrozenRegister/projects/3>
- **Status field options:** `Todo`, `In Progress`, `Done`

## Auth (one-time per machine)

Classic PATs in the OS keyring need the `project` scope:

```bash
gh auth refresh -s project
gh auth status   # verify "Token scopes" includes "project"
```

Fine-grained PATs need Organization → Projects → Read and Write.

## Common commands

```bash
# List projects for the owner
gh project list --owner FrozenRegister

# List items (returns JSON with id, content.title, content.number, status)
gh project item-list 3 --owner FrozenRegister --format json --limit 50

# Open the project board in the browser
gh project view 3 --owner FrozenRegister --web

# List status field options (one-time, to capture option IDs)
gh project field-list 3 --owner FrozenRegister
```

### Create a new draft item (back-burner work)

```bash
gh project item-create 3 --owner FrozenRegister \
  --title "Phase 1c: wire map tools into the Claude agentic loop" \
  --body "<body markdown>"
```

### Update a draft item body

Get the draft issue content ID first (`gh project item-list` returns the project-item ID `PVTI_*`, but body edits need the draft ID `DI_*` — query via `gh api graphql`):

```bash
gh api graphql -f query='query($iid:ID!){node(id:$iid){... on ProjectV2Item{content{... on DraftIssue{id} ... on Issue{id}}}}}' \
  -f iid=<PVTI_id>
# → returns e.g. "DI_xxx" for drafts, or "I_xxx" for real issues

gh project item-edit --id DI_xxx --body "<new body>"
```

### Update a real issue body (preferred for items already promoted to issues)

```bash
gh issue edit <N> --repo FrozenRegister/holmgard-lore-editor --body "<body>"
```

### Update item status

```bash
gh project item-edit \
  --id <PVTI_id> \
  --project-id PVT_kwHOEMCAyM4BZ3n- \
  --field-id <status_field_id> \
  --single-select-option-id <option_id>
```

Run `gh project field-list 3 --owner FrozenRegister --format json` to get the field ID and option IDs.

### Update a custom text/number/date/iteration field

```bash
gh project item-edit --id <PVTI_id> --project-id PVT_kwHOEMCAyM4BZ3n- \
  --field-id <field_id> --text "value"        # or --number / --date / --iteration-id
```

## New-topic convention

**When a new feature, phase, or topic is discussed and is not being implemented immediately, add it to the project as a draft issue.** The body must include everything needed to resume implementation later:

1. **Title** — concise, names the feature (e.g. `Phase 2: Worker endpoints + map tools (holmgard-lore-mcp)`)
2. **Status header** as the first line: `**Status: back-burner. Full spec: <doc-path>**` (or `**Status: active. Implementation prompt:**` if actively in progress)
3. **Implementation prompt** — a copy-pasteable prompt for a future agent session that contains all context, file paths, constraints, and acceptance criteria. If the work lives in a different repo, name that repo in the header and link to its issues/PRs as needed.
4. **Open questions** at the end (if any) — anything the user still needs to decide before implementation.

For topics that cross repos, use separate draft items per repo, each scoped to that repo's slice of the work.

Always confirm with the user before creating items, unless they explicitly say to track the topic.

## PR Quality Gate checklist

Before pushing a PR branch, ensure these CI checks will pass:

1. **CHANGELOG.md** — Every PR must modify `CHANGELOG.md` with an entry under `[Unreleased]`. Use the appropriate section (`Added`, `Changed`, `Fixed`, `Security`). Skip only for emergency hotfixes by applying the `skip-quality-checks` label.
2. **Documentation** — Every PR must either:
   - Modify at least one file under `docs/`, OR
   - Include a `## Documentation` section in the PR body describing what docs were updated
   
   If neither is present, the `check-docs` job fails. Skip only via `skip-quality-checks` label.
3. **svelte-check** — Must pass with 0 errors (warnings are OK). Before pushing, run `pnpm check` locally.
4. **Unit tests** — All unit tests must pass. Run `pnpm test` locally. Ensure `vitest.config.ts` `exclude` glob excludes `*.integration.test.*` files so they don't bleed into the unit suite.
5. **Integration tests** — All integration tests must pass. Run `pnpm test:integration`.
6. **Coverage gate** — `coverage-gap-analysis.ts` runs in CI. Both unit and integration suites must have all files ≥80% coverage.

### Common pitfalls and fixes

| Failure | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| `check-changelog` fails | `CHANGELOG.md` must be one of the changed files; add an entry |
| `check-docs` fails | Either touch a `docs/` file OR add `## Documentation` section to PR body |
| `svelte-check` type errors on discriminated unions | Use `.filter()` to narrow before accessing union-specific fields (e.g. `ops.filter(o => o.type === 'mod')` instead of `ops.find(o => o.type === 'mod')!.a`) |
| Integration tests bleeding into unit suite | Ensure `vitest.config.ts` excludes `'src/lib/**/*.integration.test.*'` |
| Unit test `__TAURI__` detection wrong | Use `delete globalThis.__TAURI__` for browser mode, not `vi.stubGlobal('__TAURI__', undefined)` — the `'in'` operator detects the stub even with undefined value |

## Session-end helper: Add-SessionSummary

At the end of a Cline/Claude session, append a draft issue to project #3 summarizing what was done and what the next session should pick up. Use the helper in `scripts/session-end.ps1`:

```powershell
. .\scripts\session-end.ps1
Add-SessionSummary `
  -Title "Short, specific title (no leading `TEST:` outside test runs)" `
  -Summary "1-3 sentences: what landed and what's the state." `
  -Todos "First follow-up", "Second follow-up" `
  -FilesTouched "src/lib/foo.ts", "src/lib/__tests__/foo.test.ts" `
  -Doc "docs/future/<feature>.md" `
  -Status Done       # or "In Progress" or "Todo" (default Todo)
```

The helper builds a body in this standard shape and creates a draft issue via `gh project item-create`:

```markdown
**Status: <Status>. Session: YYYY-MM-DD**

## Summary
<Summary>

## Todos (for next session)
- [ ] <todo 1>
- [ ] <todo 2>

## Files touched
- `path/to/file.ts`
- `path/to/test.ts`

## Context
See [docs/future/<feature>.md](docs/future/<feature>.md) for background and full spec.

Repo: <org>/<repo> | Project: https://github.com/users/FrozenRegister/projects/3
```

When the user asks for a "session summary" or "wrap up" or "log this", call `Add-SessionSummary`. Always pass `-DryRun` first in a non-obvious case to preview the body, then drop `-DryRun` to actually create it. Always confirm with the user before creating the item unless they explicitly asked for the summary.


If `Add-SessionSummary` errors with `missing required scopes [project]`, the gh token needs the **write** project scope. Run `gh auth refresh -h github.com -s project` once and complete the browser prompt.

## Cline tool failures and workarounds

Common failures observed when using Cline in this workspace. Most are Cline/Claude-specific (not general Claude API issues).

### `read_files`: `start_line`/`end_line` placement

These parameters must be **inside each file object**, not at the top level.

```diff
- { files: [{ path: "..." }], end_line: 200 }       // WRONG — Unrecognized key: "end_line"
+ { files: [{ path: "...", start_line: 1, end_line: 200 }] }  // CORRECT
```

### `run_commands`: 30-second timeout

All commands time out after **30 seconds**. This means:
- **E2E/Playwright tests cannot run locally** via `run_commands` — they need a dev server and take minutes. Treat E2E as **CI-only**; rely on the PR check-run status instead.
- **`Start-Sleep -Seconds N` where N > 25** will always time out.
- **Workaround for short waits (< 25s):** `ping -n 1 -w <ms> 1.1.1.1` (returns after the specified ms).

### `run_commands`: `args` array form vs plain string

The structured `{ command, args }` format can emit `ENOENT` on some commands (e.g. `git add`, `git commit`, `Remove-Item` with multiple args). Prefer **plain string commands** for reliability:

```diff
- { command: "git", args: ["add", "file1.ts", "file2.ts"] }  // may ENOENT
+ "git add file1.ts file2.ts"                                // reliable
```

### PowerShell: bracket paths `[id]`

PowerShell treats `[...]` as a wildcard pattern. Paths like `src/routes/entities/character/[id]/+page.svelte` fail with `Get-Content`. Always use `-LiteralPath`:

```powershell
Get-Content -LiteralPath "c:\...\character\[id]\+page.svelte"
```

### PowerShell: complex escaping in command strings

Regex, double backslashes, and embedded quotes fail with parser errors when passed inline. **Use a Python `.py` script on disk** instead:

```python
# Create fix.py with editor tool, then: python c:\...\fix.py
import pathlib
p = pathlib.Path(r'c:/path/to/file.ts')
t = p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
t = t.replace("old", "new")
p.write_text(t, encoding='utf-8')
```

### `editor`: exact `old_text` matching

`old_text` must match **byte-for-byte** including whitespace. Indentation mismatches cause silent "text not found" failures. If a replacement fails, fall back to a **Python script** (see previous section).

### `search_codebase`: 30-second timeout

Broad or complex regex patterns can timeout. Narrow patterns are more reliable. If a search times out, break it into smaller, more specific searches.

### `fetch_web_content`: GitHub Actions logs require auth

GitHub Actions job logs return "Sign in to view logs." You can only read **job status/conclusion** via the check-runs API. Detailed test failure output requires alternative approaches (e.g. looking at the PR diff for obvious bugs, or using `get_commit` with `detail: "full_patch"` to reason about changes).

### `github__pull_request_read`: method names

For the MCP tool, use camelCase method names like `get_check_runs`, not `getCheckRuns`. The method parameter takes values from an enum (e.g. `"get"`, `"get_diff"`, `"get_files"`, `"get_commits"`, `"get_review_comments"`, `"get_reviews"`, `"get_comments"`, `"get_check_runs"`, `"get_status"`).

### Playwright `page.route()` ordering

When multiple `page.route()` calls target the same URL pattern, the **last registered** handler wins for matched requests. A `route.fulfill()` in an earlier handler will intercept the request before later handlers run. If you need to override an earlier route, either call the later route after all earlier ones, or avoid calling the earlier route altogether.

### Playwright toast selectors in this project

Toasts render inside a `<div class="toast-stack">` in `src/routes/+layout.svelte`. Use:

```typescript
page.locator('.toast-stack').getByText('message text')
```

Not `.toast-container` (that class does not exist in this project).

